Edition No. 41 — Wednesday, 11 November 2026

10 stories: 'Properly before the court': the High Court rejects the State's objection and clears the way for the 18 treason accused to argue for bail, as the sedition bail plea slips to Monday (lead); 1,247 in Kitwe and 311 in Ndola: the Copperbelt minister orders 1,558 'excess' teachers out to rural schools, as Musokotwane says there will be no teacher recruitment in 2027; Back after 15 years: Canada reopens its High Commission in Lusaka with US$10.5 million in new support and a cooperation agreement that names critical minerals first; 'Business should not be done through the courts': Hichilema and Trafigura's chairman agree a reset, with legal disputes to settle and concentrate exports in the President's sights; 16,000 megawatts on paper, 4,722 on the grid: Kapala visits the ERB and calls in the developers whose signed power deals cannot find the money to build; A plea agreement, not yet filed: the State says it has a deal with David Kazadi and two co-accused in the obscene-material case, and the magistrate gives it until close of business; Daka, Lameck Banda and Gamphani Lungu are out of both Burundi games: three injuries, three replacements, and three Europe-based arrivals land in Ndola on the same afternoon; A K93 million KCM deal, 40% of the referees' arrears paid and Asanović's debt stretched to 2028: the Times of Zambia sets out FAZ's own account of 18 months of balance-sheet repair; 'Easier to ship from Zambia to London than to Ghana': AfroQuality opens its first Lusaka store at Arcades to sell African brands across African borders; Kalambo, Chishimba and the Mwela rock art: the Culture minister visits Kasama and says the Northern Circuit needs roads, marketing and money to help reach five million visitors.

